WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.589 --> 00:00:12.200
Right.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:26.960
So, let me begin by taking you back in time.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.590
The time is now 10 past 5.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:35.680
On the 28th December, 1978.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:36.970
(music)

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:41.250
In the skies above Portland Oregon, is a DC
8.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.770
There are 189 souls on board.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:49.550
Going to land.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:57.500
So, as they are coming to land, they start
to lower the undercarriage.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.110
There is a big loud thumb.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:05.280
They can hear and feel.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.850
It is not much of a approach.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:15.049
They are going to go back upto 5000 feet.

00:01:15.049 --> 00:01:21.749
We have a landing gear problem.

00:01:21.749 --> 00:01:25.380
Landing gear problems aren't a big deal.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.719
They can land with any undercarriage at all.

00:01:29.719 --> 00:01:41.869
It is normal for landing gear failures that
happen every year.

00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:43.549
Nothing interesting happens.

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:44.549
Nobody dies.

00:01:44.549 --> 00:01:56.770
It is a huge surprise when after some regular
ordinary radio traffic, Portland hears this

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:59.289
one.

00:01:59.289 --> 00:02:01.130
United 173 heavy.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:02.130
Maydy.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.869
The engines are flaming out, we are going
down.

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:06.929
We are not going to be able to make the airport.

00:02:06.929 --> 00:02:15.719
They impact with powerlines.

00:02:15.719 --> 00:02:22.670
That's downtown Portland.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.670
That's what it ended up looking like.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:27.550
Only 10 people died.

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:30.560
24 were seriously injured.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.209
Most of it was because they hit a tree.

00:02:33.209 --> 00:02:36.159
It was a small wooded area.

00:02:36.159 --> 00:02:39.360
They were very lucky.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:47.569
There was a rescue team on an exercise when
they hit the ground.

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:50.980
So, this is air crashes.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:52.570
In case you got confused.

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:54.760
That the last picture was a makefile.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.250
I'm Dave Cridland.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:09.069
I have been working in and around the internet
around 1996.

00:03:09.069 --> 00:03:16.459
I work for Surevine.

00:03:16.459 --> 00:03:23.079
My colleague Lloyd over there also speaking,
works with us as well.

00:03:23.079 --> 00:03:26.629
And we do social stuff on the web.

00:03:26.629 --> 00:03:32.629
For people who really care about security.

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:37.530
When I got into air crashes or reading them.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:38.670
And reading the reports.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.689
This was way before they were all on the web.

00:03:40.689 --> 00:03:42.160
Which they are now.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.940
They have pretty animations.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:54.709
I got into them, a company I worked for 15
years ago, would hand you aircraft air reports.

00:03:54.709 --> 00:04:00.810
Read this one, it is great.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:04.190
I don't do anything with aircraft.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:05.880
I do software.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:12.219
Aircraft are large systems with interacting
components.

00:04:12.219 --> 00:04:13.790
So is the software that we built.

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:18.510
It was Javascript.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:25.759
And, the kinds of areas that you get.

00:04:25.759 --> 00:04:29.840
Sorry, skipped a bit.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:37.800
What we do to avoid errors is we deal with
redundancy and recoupling.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:43.380
The failures we get are error chains.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.569
Cascade failures.

00:04:45.569 --> 00:04:47.770
Same thing.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:52.729
And even the name that we use is the same.

00:04:52.729 --> 00:05:00.870
With aircraft, every time an aircraft doesn't
function as it should, there is a detailed

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:08.780
investigation analysing every cause and all
possible recommendations afterwards.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:15.360
It is not quite the same in software.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.129
(applause)

00:05:18.129 --> 00:05:26.310
So, I have read dozens of reports and summaries.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:30.430
I'm still not scared to fly.

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:34.310
I took 2 flights here.

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:36.900
I had to change at Schiphol.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:46.389
There are 2 accident reports that leave me
feeling scared about everything.

00:05:46.389 --> 00:05:49.110
How many people flew here?

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:50.110
That's great.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:53.069
How many are thinking of flying back.

00:05:53.069 --> 00:05:54.840
Okay.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.810
We'll change that!

00:05:56.810 --> 00:06:00.690
So, what happened with United 173.

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:05.550
Why did that plane crash?

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:07.230
This might give you a hint.

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:08.580
A small one.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:15.349
When it took off from Denver, Co, it had 46700
pounds of fuel.

00:06:15.349 --> 00:06:20.770
They like the imperial measurements.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:34.360
When it was on the approach to Portland, we
can measure how it flew, it had 14800 pounds

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.300
of fuel left.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:45.879
We know that, the undercarriage was down,
flaps was down.

00:06:45.879 --> 00:06:58.960
It is burning 2200 pounds per minute.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:03.180
He sits behind the pilot.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:07.750
He has the full readouts of everything.

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:16.800
The 2 pilots, captain, first officer, they
were at the figure.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:22.750
In order to check exactly how much fuel you
have, you ask the flight engineer.

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:25.569
They had a headline.

00:07:25.569 --> 00:07:30.060
By 17:46 they have 5000.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:32.970
And the flight engineer is reporting this
to the first officer.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:36.770
Who is the pilot flying.

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:40.729
The captain is sorting out problems.

00:07:40.729 --> 00:07:53.669
And the pilot in charge, captain, tells to
prepare for landing with 4000 pounds of fuel.

00:07:53.669 --> 00:07:57.430
You can see, that is minutes after he had
5000.

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:59.289
Something wrong here.

00:07:59.289 --> 00:08:03.340
The first officer requires a fuel check.

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:07.080
He is told 4000.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:14.090
The captain tells Portland, we are going to
land with 3000 pounds of fuel.

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:16.560
This is not right.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.889
6 minutes past 6.

00:08:20.889 --> 00:08:26.909
The first officer says: We are going to lose
an engine buddy.

00:08:26.909 --> 00:08:29.250
Why?

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:31.590
Fuel.

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:35.110
Well, activate the cross streets.

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:39.150
To shuffle fuel between tanks.

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:46.170
Then they think it is a good idea to ask for
landing clearance a minute later.

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:54.710
Couple of minutes later the flight engineer
is reporting only 1000 pounds.

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:58.350
Mayday is declared at 18.13.

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:06.260
With the engines running out.

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:13.030
The report basically said, this accident exemplifies
a recurring problem with management of crew

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:14.750
resources.

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:18.060
This is an odd phrase to use.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:26.620
The blame was assigned to the captain, who
was stripped of his license and never flew

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:28.330
again.

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:32.400
The contributing factor was given as crew
failure.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:36.870
They are not saying the landing gear was a
problem, the fuel.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:42.640
They say, this was a crew problem.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:43.890
This is rather unfortunate.

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:49.020
The flight engineer was one of those killed
in impact.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:55.460
Before I go on to other crashes which this
is referring to.

00:09:55.460 --> 00:09:59.790
There is a top aircraft safety tip.

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:04.480
Where is the safest place?

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:05.880
The passenger seats.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:12.920
As a crew you are must more likely to die.

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:17.510
Be a passenger.

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:21.500
The related crashes which are similar.

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:27.760
1963 Tupelov 124 in the Neva River.

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:30.920
There was a landing gear problem on take off.

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:35.930
They decided they would circle to use up fuel.

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:39.730
In the days before the aircraft dumped it.

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:43.500
They looped too many times.

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:46.860
No casualties.

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:52.770
They didn't evacuate the aircraft, they towed
it.

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:57.850
1969 Scandinavian.

00:10:57.850 --> 00:10:59.450
Failed to watch the altitude.

00:10:59.450 --> 00:11:02.480
15 people killed.

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:07.420
1972, Eastern 401.

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:10.710
Famous crash.

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:11.770
Landing gear problem.

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:14.920
They disenaged the auto pilot by accident.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:22.240
Causing 99 fatalities when they crashed into
the Everglades.

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:24.360
And then the big one.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:27.420
Tenerife airport disaster.

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:33.440
27 March 1977.

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:46.760
I have never heard properly the opening lines
to Knowing me, knowing you before.

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:51.600
They were number 1 at the time of the crash.

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:54.040
It is 6 in the evening.

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:58.990
We are at Los Rodeos Tenerife.

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:03.980
The airport name is changed to Tenerife north.

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:10.010
Flight numbers are tend to be put out of service.

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:12.600
They did it with this airport as well.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:15.390
They built a new airport.

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:17.440
This is a cascade failure.

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:18.920
A chain of errors.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:24.120
Leading to a fatal disaster.

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:27.990
It starts off with a bomb that explodes in
Las Palmas.

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:31.350
The airport in Gran Canaria.

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:37.440
By seperatists, protesting against the Franco
regime.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:42.670
Franco dies later that year.

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:45.920
And there was another bomb warning given.

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:47.420
Although there wasn't another bomb.

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:56.970
They closed the airport and diverted all flights
to 

00:12:56.970 --> 00:12:59.550
Los Rodeos.

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:00.870
It is relatively high altitude.

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:01.870
600 meters.

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:11.270
It has a problem with dense low clouds.

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:13.190
It had this at the time.

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:16.160
They had an unusual amount of traffic.

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:18.020
Normally they didn't see much.

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:23.130
Now their airport is full.

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:29.130
The 2 aircraft in question are the Klm flight
4805.

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:32.350
With 248 people.

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:37.540
And PanAm with 396 people.

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:39.890
These both 747.

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:45.530
Both flew international.

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:53.300
So, and I pulled this diagram of Wikipedia.

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:56.060
And adjusted it to fit.

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:57.750
This is what happened.

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:02.070
The Klm flight was first to take off.

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:04.490
It was blocking all of the others.

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:12.800
The apron where the aircraft park was jammed
full with aircraft.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:15.100
They couldn't use the normal taxi way.

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:19.510
That runs across the top.

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:23.120
So the Klm flight is first to take off.

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:28.760
It runs to the back of the entry, turns around.

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:32.480
And the Pan Am follows quickly afterwards.

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:35.110
With the intend to turn off.

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:40.440
They are confused which taxi to use.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:52.060
They are told when asked for clarification
to use the 3rd one.

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:54.510
For whatever reason, you can see the Pan Am
misses.

00:14:54.510 --> 00:15:01.470
It is clear they didn't know where they were
on the runway.

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:02.470
That's okay.

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:06.100
The control tower knows they are on the runway.

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:16.160
The Klm flight piloted by a very experienced
captain.

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:17.240
The chief instructor at the end.

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:21.780
He gets lined up.

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:23.250
We don't have clearance.

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:29.380
Air traffic control clearance.

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:32.780
So he says to go ahead and ask.

00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:37.340
And so they asked the tower, they give the
clearance.

00:15:37.340 --> 00:15:39.480
Atc.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:44.170
They repeat the instructions back.

00:15:44.170 --> 00:15:51.570
Now what happens is something that is really
quite seriously horrible.

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:56.630
Because the tower responds with okay.

00:15:56.630 --> 00:16:03.530
And what you see there is the normal type
is what both aircraft can hear.

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:10.990
Due to radio interference only Pan Am can
hear it.

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:23.050
Klm hears: Okay.

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:28.730
They don't hear: Stand by for take off, I
will call you.

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:36.310
They don't hear: No, uhm.

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:43.160
They do both hear papa alpha one seven three
six report the runway clear.

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:50.860
I don't know if Klm thought it was clear.

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:53.110
The tower was reporting runway clear.

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:54.110
It never calls.

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:55.110
It could be confusing.

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:56.110
Either way, they push forward the engines.

00:16:56.110 --> 00:17:11.220
The flight engineer says, the American is
clear.

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:21.009
And gets a definite jawel from 
the captain.

00:17:21.009 --> 00:17:28.390
Jawel for those who don't speak Dutch: Absofuckinglutely.

00:17:28.390 --> 00:17:30.810
The flight engineer...

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:38.059
They can't see eachother.

00:17:38.059 --> 00:17:39.950
Suddenly they start to see the lights.

00:17:39.950 --> 00:17:42.720
He says, there is.

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:47.200
That goddam son of a bitch is coming.

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:49.899
They turn it.

00:17:49.899 --> 00:17:51.809
Engines on full.

00:17:51.809 --> 00:17:54.059
Rather unusual manoeuvre.

00:17:54.059 --> 00:18:02.830
And tries to get into the exit 4.

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:08.480
As they Klm flight rolls down they call V1.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:13.529
If you have been in an aircraft.

00:18:13.529 --> 00:18:20.200
You know where the aircraft can tip back,
rotate.

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:21.200
That's V1.

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:24.830
They are V1 at that point.

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:28.059
It means their nose is pointed up.

00:18:28.059 --> 00:18:33.720
They cannot see the runway if it is clear.

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:37.190
The captain sees the Pan Am flight.

00:18:37.190 --> 00:18:41.679
And pulls back even harder.

00:18:41.679 --> 00:18:48.129
Where the rear of the aircraft strikes the
runway.

00:18:48.129 --> 00:18:51.650
With a big hole.

00:18:51.650 --> 00:18:53.299
He manages to get it airborn.

00:18:53.299 --> 00:18:57.970
But they are full of fuel.

00:18:57.970 --> 00:19:02.870
They cannot clear.

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:05.659
You get this impact.

00:19:05.659 --> 00:19:10.220
This is what the transcript says.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:13.899
Explamation, expletive.

00:19:13.899 --> 00:19:19.900
That's actually the last that anyone will
hear from the Klm flight.

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:22.779
It impacts.

00:19:22.779 --> 00:19:24.769
More or less breaking apart.

00:19:24.769 --> 00:19:28.210
All the way down the runway.

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:30.330
Everyone is killed on that.

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:31.330
Instantly.

00:19:31.330 --> 00:19:37.610
The fact it is full of fuel, the fire is horrific.

00:19:37.610 --> 00:19:41.680
The fire crew immediately head to the Klm
flight.

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:44.680
They are not aware of 2 aircraft.

00:19:44.680 --> 00:19:52.090
They mistake Pan Am for another piece of wreckage.

00:19:52.090 --> 00:19:58.190
When they get there, 61 survivors have escaped
by themselves.

00:19:58.190 --> 00:20:05.080
335 people are in the aircraft, waiting what
to do.

00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:11.150
A top tip when you get in, listen to the safety
briefing, plan your exit.

00:20:11.150 --> 00:20:15.200
It may come as a surprise you might need to
use it.

00:20:15.200 --> 00:20:22.049
So, the airline industry started using crew
resource management.

00:20:22.049 --> 00:20:28.289
Which is a doctrine where everyone in the
crew maintains situational awareness.

00:20:28.289 --> 00:20:30.250
Knowing what is going on.

00:20:30.250 --> 00:20:34.499
Knowing there is another plane.

00:20:34.499 --> 00:20:38.559
All this information must be shared in the
team.

00:20:38.559 --> 00:20:40.830
Planning decision making should be shared.

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:44.460
Although you have a single leader who makes
the decision.

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:51.789
Every one is voiced their objections.

00:20:51.789 --> 00:20:57.070
Communication inside and outside the cockpit
are vital.

00:20:57.070 --> 00:20:59.740
And inside and outside the team.

00:20:59.740 --> 00:21:02.289
And good teamwork.

00:21:02.289 --> 00:21:06.019
Everyone gets on with the tasks they need
to.

00:21:06.019 --> 00:21:10.890
Rather than trying to get to do the same task.

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:13.450
This isn't a leadership talk.

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:23.740
This is a talk about a lot of people dying
a hideous death.

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:26.990
Leading people is just one person's job.

00:21:26.990 --> 00:21:30.049
And effective teamwork is everybody's job.

00:21:30.049 --> 00:21:35.809
The entire team needs to be involved.

00:21:35.809 --> 00:21:45.690
So, crew management is taken on by United
in 1980, followed by talks by Nasa.

00:21:45.690 --> 00:21:48.970
In their Apollo program.

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:57.740
It was thought this was going to help minimize
crew problems, failures.

00:21:57.740 --> 00:21:59.320
In the future.

00:21:59.320 --> 00:22:07.799
So, what happens if a crew faces what normally
would be a non survivable incident?

00:22:07.799 --> 00:22:12.280
They have excellent crew resource management.

00:22:12.280 --> 00:22:22.330
So, this would be United 232.

00:22:22.330 --> 00:22:28.740
On 19 July 1989, at 3.15 in the afternoon.

00:22:28.740 --> 00:22:31.850
Near Sioux City, Iowa.

00:22:31.850 --> 00:22:36.640
A 3-engined airliner.

00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:38.779
Was flying from Denver to Chicago.

00:22:38.779 --> 00:22:40.240
296 souls.

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:49.559
An unusual number was children.

00:22:49.559 --> 00:22:56.390
After 3.15, the rear central engine blew.

00:22:56.390 --> 00:23:03.889
They fan, main turbine disk shattered into
several pieces.

00:23:03.889 --> 00:23:08.260
And the pieces went through the tailpipe assembly.

00:23:08.260 --> 00:23:14.769
What that left United 232 with was 2 problems.

00:23:14.769 --> 00:23:23.249
Firstly the aircraft had a tendency to turn
to the right because of the damage.

00:23:23.249 --> 00:23:26.690
Left alone it would flip itself over completely.

00:23:26.690 --> 00:23:30.690
You don't want to do that.

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:33.120
That is not a major problem.

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:35.441
You have the aircraft controls to compensate.

00:23:35.441 --> 00:23:39.250
Move it back.

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:43.309
This would be a problem if they lost all the
hydrolics.

00:23:43.309 --> 00:23:45.240
Which they had.

00:23:45.240 --> 00:23:49.279
All 3 hydrolic systems were made inoperative.

00:23:49.279 --> 00:23:58.159
Each is driven by an engine.

00:23:58.159 --> 00:24:03.529
All of the hydrolic fluid drained out of the
system.

00:24:03.529 --> 00:24:10.619
The flight engineer on the radio says, this
is united 232.

00:24:10.619 --> 00:24:13.159
We blew nummer 2, we lost all hydraulics.

00:24:13.159 --> 00:24:20.570
We are only able to control with asymmetrical
power settings.

00:24:20.570 --> 00:24:27.360
They had no rudders or speed breaks or even
a little break on the wheels.

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:30.879
Should they land.

00:24:30.879 --> 00:24:35.149
The maintenance then had a really interesting
amusing conversation.

00:24:35.149 --> 00:24:41.190
You still have 1 and 3?

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:50.580
No, you have 1 and 2?

00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:56.090
This was calculated as being a 10 to the 9th
to 1 chance.

00:24:56.090 --> 00:25:04.429
The United have checklists for all, except
this one.

00:25:04.429 --> 00:25:09.169
It is deemed impossible and unsurvivable.

00:25:09.169 --> 00:25:12.820
The captain said, we are trying to go straight.

00:25:12.820 --> 00:25:16.440
We're not having much luck.

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:20.470
The crew sorted out the engine problem.

00:25:20.470 --> 00:25:24.159
Within 14 seconds.

00:25:24.159 --> 00:25:28.299
It was then that they realised the plane wasn't
reacting.

00:25:28.299 --> 00:25:31.470
They started talking all 3 themselves.

00:25:31.470 --> 00:25:34.350
Every operation they were doing.

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:36.799
Continue using the controls.

00:25:36.799 --> 00:25:43.750
In case it had effects.

00:25:43.750 --> 00:25:50.799
Everything they have to do is talk through
as an entire team before they make a decision.

00:25:50.799 --> 00:25:57.909
Haynes: We didn't do this thing on my last
performance check.

00:25:57.909 --> 00:26:02.360
Then something remarkable happens.

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:04.900
This is real Hollywood moment.

00:26:04.900 --> 00:26:08.760
One of the passengers comes up to help.

00:26:08.760 --> 00:26:14.190
I'll introduce myself.

00:26:14.190 --> 00:26:18.549
The passenger introduces himself, Denny Fitch.

00:26:18.549 --> 00:26:26.059
He has been working for the last 3-4 weeks
as United DC 10 flight instructor.

00:26:26.059 --> 00:26:31.230
He is the 1 person that you really want in
the cockpit.

00:26:31.230 --> 00:26:33.399
This is in part.

00:26:33.399 --> 00:26:38.960
He is familiar with another crash.

00:26:38.960 --> 00:26:49.429
Japan Airlines 123.

00:26:49.429 --> 00:26:53.740
This is the most serious single incident air
disaster after.

00:26:53.740 --> 00:26:55.999
1985, 6.24.

00:26:55.999 --> 00:27:01.230
It is getting dark.

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:03.830
The plane took off from Tokyo.

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:11.919
A 747 short range which carries extra people.

00:27:11.919 --> 00:27:17.789
A tragedy in itself.

00:27:17.789 --> 00:27:25.610
As they flew upto altitude, a bad repair to
a bulkhead causes decompression.

00:27:25.610 --> 00:27:29.210
I shall use my laserpointer.

00:27:29.210 --> 00:27:31.809
Can you see there?

00:27:31.809 --> 00:27:35.100
There is a bit missing.

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:40.570
The vertical stabilizor has been blown off.

00:27:40.570 --> 00:27:47.130
That has taken out all of the hydraulics.

00:27:47.130 --> 00:27:50.490
They managed 35 minutes of flight time.

00:27:50.490 --> 00:27:53.049
In a very unstable aircraft.

00:27:53.049 --> 00:27:57.340
They were not only missing control service.

00:27:57.340 --> 00:28:01.340
They were missing large chunks of the aircraft.

00:28:01.340 --> 00:28:06.659
They crashed in a remote area.

00:28:06.659 --> 00:28:12.480
Initial reports suggested no survivors.

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:19.149
One of the 4 survivors they found the next
morning said that the heard the helicopter

00:28:19.149 --> 00:28:25.580
circling, see the lights and it flew off.

00:28:25.580 --> 00:28:31.909
All the moaning from the other survivors also
faded to nothing.

00:28:31.909 --> 00:28:32.909
As you might guess.

00:28:32.909 --> 00:28:36.830
This is the other one that really freaks me
out.

00:28:36.830 --> 00:28:40.070
Back to United 232.

00:28:40.070 --> 00:28:43.000
I'm looking shaky on time.

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:47.529
- They are also running late.

00:28:47.529 --> 00:28:48.529
- All right.

00:28:48.529 --> 00:28:52.570
In that case, I'll take ages!

00:28:52.570 --> 00:28:57.279
United 232.

00:28:57.279 --> 00:29:00.559
Haynes is in no illusions.

00:29:00.559 --> 00:29:05.049
He is talking to the cabin crew.

00:29:05.049 --> 00:29:10.369
I can't remember the name.

00:29:10.369 --> 00:29:13.840
She is talking about whether or not they are
going to evacuate.

00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:22.059
If we are at the front I'll tell you.

00:29:22.059 --> 00:29:26.460
He comments, won't this be a fun landing?

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:32.509
The air crew talk about every decision.

00:29:32.509 --> 00:29:40.860
One of the decisions they talk about is how
and whether to lower the landing gear.

00:29:40.860 --> 00:29:45.460
An aircraft can land without a landing gear.

00:29:45.460 --> 00:29:49.409
On a soft surface it is preferably.

00:29:49.409 --> 00:29:58.120
Otherwise it will cause more damage.

00:29:58.120 --> 00:30:00.629
They decided unanimously to lower it.

00:30:00.629 --> 00:30:03.559
There are 2 mechanisms.

00:30:03.559 --> 00:30:06.830
They decide to crank it down.

00:30:06.830 --> 00:30:14.879
Which will release some extra hydraulic fluid.

00:30:14.879 --> 00:30:20.960
So they get closer and closer to Sioux city
airport.

00:30:20.960 --> 00:30:27.380
Under the very excellent guidance of Kevin.

00:30:27.380 --> 00:30:34.580
Who is a flight controller who moved to Sioux
City because he was at a busier airport and

00:30:34.580 --> 00:30:37.360
didn't like the stress.

00:30:37.360 --> 00:30:43.450
You are cleared to land on any runway.

00:30:43.450 --> 00:30:45.909
Haynes: Typical humor.

00:30:45.909 --> 00:30:53.190
You want to be particular and make it a runway?

00:30:53.190 --> 00:30:59.619
They line up for runway 22, a closed runway.

00:30:59.619 --> 00:31:02.840
You cannot see the whole of the runway from
the tower.

00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:09.640
It is also where they have all the emergency
equipment parked.

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:14.950
They moved it out of the way.

00:31:14.950 --> 00:31:24.190
As they are coming in to land, it is incredible
as far as an airport.

00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:34.190
As they come in, a normal landing in a Dc
10 is 140 knots, 200 feet per minute.

00:31:34.190 --> 00:31:39.009
They are coming in at 215 knots, and increasing.

00:31:39.009 --> 00:31:41.889
And 1850 feet per minute.

00:31:41.889 --> 00:31:44.389
Nearly 10 times faster than they should be.

00:31:44.389 --> 00:31:46.460
Is that going to fail?

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:57.350
See, I shouldn't have controlled it with Java
script.

00:31:57.350 --> 00:32:02.190
Let's try it again.

00:32:02.190 --> 00:32:03.399
Sorry.

00:32:03.399 --> 00:32:30.470
This is a similar view than what you get from
the tower.

00:32:30.470 --> 00:32:39.139
So, Kevin sees that from the tower.

00:32:39.139 --> 00:32:44.230
And in his own words, he went down the stairs
and had a little cry.

00:32:44.230 --> 00:32:48.779
Amazingly, there were survivors.

00:32:48.779 --> 00:32:52.200
112 were killed.

00:32:52.200 --> 00:33:00.179
There is a complication how you measure fatalities.

00:33:00.179 --> 00:33:01.940
Instantly 1 was killed.

00:33:01.940 --> 00:33:04.309
184 survived.

00:33:04.309 --> 00:33:06.690
Almost 2/3.

00:33:06.690 --> 00:33:20.619
There was a united captain, one of the very
few survivers from First Class.

00:33:20.619 --> 00:33:24.489
You can't climb out of an aircraft window.

00:33:24.489 --> 00:33:27.950
Yes you can.

00:33:27.950 --> 00:33:32.470
It comes in, the right wing tips at the last
minute.

00:33:32.470 --> 00:33:36.590
They couldn't control this.

00:33:36.590 --> 00:33:41.759
The right wing hits, spills fuel.

00:33:41.759 --> 00:33:43.309
The tail brakes off.

00:33:43.309 --> 00:33:47.049
The aircraft flips up.

00:33:47.049 --> 00:33:50.730
Loses the cockpit, starts breaking apart.

00:33:50.730 --> 00:33:54.379
It looks like a cartwheel.

00:33:54.379 --> 00:33:59.080
The whole thing is upside down.

00:33:59.080 --> 00:34:08.290
The passengers help the crew as much as the
crew helped the passengers.

00:34:08.290 --> 00:34:11.670
They were unable to release themselves.

00:34:11.670 --> 00:34:18.440
1 passenger out of the hole of the side, went
back in, because he heard a baby cry.

00:34:18.440 --> 00:34:24.610
It was thrown into the overhead luggage bins.

00:34:24.610 --> 00:34:30.260
What happened to the crew.

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:34.480
The cockpit wreckage was found over half way.

00:34:34.480 --> 00:34:42.100
It goes down to there.

00:34:42.100 --> 00:34:46.280
This is a part of the plane that should be
10 feet high.

00:34:46.280 --> 00:34:55.850
They found it because the flight engineer
had his arms stuck up through the wires.

00:34:55.850 --> 00:34:58.560
There were people inside.

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:08.440
They found the entire flight crew alive, battered
and injured, but all of them alive.

00:35:08.440 --> 00:35:13.180
Amazingly, all of the crew survived and returned
to work.

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:16.810
Most of them to work.

00:35:16.810 --> 00:35:22.980
Denny was sitting behind operating the throttles.

00:35:22.980 --> 00:35:25.660
He would never fly again.

00:35:25.660 --> 00:35:29.480
He managed to 11 months later.

00:35:29.480 --> 00:35:32.320
On DC 10's.

00:35:32.320 --> 00:35:35.160
- 30 seconds.

00:35:35.160 --> 00:35:37.860
- That's good.

00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:47.290
So, these quotes from a talk by Hanes.

00:35:47.290 --> 00:35:49.490
You can find it on Youtube.

00:35:49.490 --> 00:35:57.690
Up until 1980 we worked on the concept that
the captain was the authority.

00:35:57.690 --> 00:36:00.290
And we had a 103 years of flying experience.

00:36:00.290 --> 00:36:01.980
There in the cockpit.

00:36:01.980 --> 00:36:04.670
Trying to get that airplane on the ground.

00:36:04.670 --> 00:36:07.130
Not one minute of which we had practiced.

00:36:07.130 --> 00:36:10.040
Any one of us.

00:36:10.040 --> 00:36:16.040
If I hadn't used Crm, it is a cinch we wouldn't
have made it.

00:36:16.040 --> 00:36:23.830
The work we do for most of us, will never
involve human lives, anything like that.

00:36:23.830 --> 00:36:29.940
But, nevertheless, you can learn a lot from
how to do teamwork the aviation way.

00:36:29.940 --> 00:36:30.940
Thanks very much.

00:36:30.940 --> 00:36:31.230
(applause)

